 This install applies to "Wing Commander" (c) 1994 Origin.
 Disk versions (SPS #824 and #1657) and the CD32 Dangerous Streets /
 Wing Commander compilation are supported in English and German.
 Wing Commander Enhanced 2.0b (17.11.2026)
 Original install: Psygore. Version 2.0b improvements: Vesuri.

 Requirements:
 -------------
 WHDLoad 17 or newer and xfddecrunch must be available in the command path.
 xfddecrunch is supplied by the XFD package (aminet:util/pack/xfdmaster.lha).
 Use Expert installation mode to select a floppy drive other than DF0:.

 Disk/OCS: 512 KB Chip RAM and 1 MB other RAM, plus about 2.5 MB for PRELOAD.
 CD32/AGA: 640 KB Chip RAM and 1.4 MB other RAM, plus about 4.1 MB for PRELOAD.
 Additional free memory is needed for WHDLoad and the enhanced renderer.
 For enhanced play, use a 68020 or better with Fast RAM. The tested OCS
 configuration starts at 512 KB Chip RAM and 4 MB Fast RAM including PRELOAD.
 Rendering speed depends on the processor and scene; 50 FPS is not guaranteed.

 The OCS slave requires an A500 Kickstart 1.3 image:
   Devs:Kickstarts/kick34005.A500
   Devs:Kickstarts/kick34005.A500.RTB
 The CD32 slave requires an A1200 or A4000 Kickstart 3.1 image:
   Devs:Kickstarts/kick40068.A1200 and kick40068.A1200.RTB, or
   Devs:Kickstarts/kick40068.A4000 and kick40068.A4000.RTB
 Supply your own ROM image, extracted using GrabKick or a similar tool.
 The matching RTB files are available in aminet:util/boot/skick346.lha.
 WHDLoad rejects an incorrect or altered ROM image.

 Installation:
 -------------
 Run Install and select the disk or CD32 edition. The disk installer requests
 WingDisk1, WingDisk2 and WingDisk3, identifies English/German, and unpacks the
 executable. The CD32 installer copies Wingco from the disc named Bundle: and
 unpacks both language executables. Keep the two editions in separate drawers.
 Existing destination drawers are preserved; choose a new destination instead.
 Manual and QuickDocEN/DE/FR/IT are included, together with the original icons.

 Features and options:
 ---------------------
 Quit with '*' (PrtSc), or select another key using WHDLoad's QUITKEY tooltype.
 A mouse in port 0 moves the pointer in the rooms and menus, in every mode.
 Its left button works as fire: it clicks, skips and continues sequences, and
 fires the guns in flight for as long as you hold it, as on the PC. On CD32 it
 also moves the selection box when you enter your name, and the right button
 works as Return there, so a name can be entered with the mouse alone. The
 joystick, CD32 pad and keys work as before.
 In flight the mouse steers as in the PC version: move the pointer right of
 the gunsight to turn right and below it to pull the nose up. The further it
 is from the gunsight, the faster the turn, and the ship keeps turning until
 you move it back. The pointer appears when you move the mouse and hides after
 10 seconds without mouse movement. Using the joystick or keys to steer
 re-centres it.
 The other mouse buttons in flight work as on the PC: hold the right button
 and move the mouse forward or back to change speed, or left or right to
 roll, four times as fast as with the keys; the pointer stays where it is
 meanwhile. Double-click the right button and hold it to fire the
 afterburner. Press both buttons together, or the middle button, to fire the
 selected missile or drop a mine. The right button also works as Esc in the
 cutscenes: it skips them as Esc does.
 The mouse speed is set in the WHDLoad splash window or with CUSTOM5 (below):
 1.0x is two mouse counts a screen pixel, as the PC's default; 0.5x to 4.0x
 scale every use of the mouse alike. Off removes the mouse support
 altogether: the game then reads only the joystick, CD32 pad and keys, as in
 Psygore's slave.
 On CD32, saving a pilot in the barracks also saves the Training Simulator high
 scores, as Bed_N.hi beside the Bed_N save, and loading that pilot restores
 them. Saves without this file still load, with the game's usual scores.
 The installed icon enables enhanced mode and DCACHE by default. NOCACHE can
 be used to disable caching. On CD32 every 2.0b change works in English and
 German. On disks, the 2.0b changes apply to the English SPS #1657 version;
 other disk versions keep the 1.0 behaviour.

 CUSTOM1: 0 = English, 1 = German.
 CUSTOM2: 0 = cheats off, 1 = cheat mode, 2 = invincible cheat mode.
 In cheat mode hold SHIFT and press:
   F1  music on/off                 F6  self-destruct
   F2  sound effects on/off         F7  cockpit damage graphics
   F3  video images on/off          F8  game information
   F4  message speed                F9  pause for screenshots
   F5  destroy locked target        F10 eject
 CUSTOM3 selects a system (cheat mode required):
   0 Default       1 Enyo          2 McAuliffe       3 Gateway
   4 Gimle         5 Brimstone     6 Chengdu         7 Dakota
   8 Port Hedland  9 Kurasawa     10 Rostov         11 Hubble's Star
  12 Venice       13 Hell's Kitchen
 CUSTOM4: 0 = mission 1, 1 = mission 2, 2 = mission 3 (cheat mode required).

 CUSTOM5 combines three options; add their values:
   World speed:          0 = 1.0x (10 updates/s)   2 = 1.2x   4 = 1.5x
                         6 = 2.0x         8 = CPU-dependent
   Enhanced framerate:  +1 = smooth in-between pictures in flight
   Mouse speed:          0 = 1.0x        16 = off         32 = 0.5x
                        48 = 0.75x       64 = 1.5x        80 = 2.0x
                        96 = 3.0x       112 = 4.0x
 The installed icon uses CUSTOM5=1: enhanced framerate at 1.0x, mouse at 1.0x.
 For example CUSTOM5=65 is the same with the mouse at 1.5x. Without CUSTOM5
 the world runs at 1.0x without the enhanced framerate.
 CPU-dependent is the original game, whose world advances once per picture,
 so its speed depends on the processor; the enhanced framerate needs a fixed
 speed and is not used with it.
 A fixed world speed keeps flight at that speed on every fast enough machine.
 Without the enhanced framerate it draws one picture per world update.
 World speed applies to flight and to the intro's battle and asteroid field;
 credit text and music keep their timing, as do rooms and cinematics.
 OCS: a 68000/68010 or a machine without suitable Fast RAM always uses the
 original behaviour. Slow expansion RAM does not qualify.
 CD32: CPU-dependent uses Psygore's normal mode; his unlimited, relaxed and slow
 modes and the old original-conversion option have been removed.
 When updating a 1.0 icon: CD32 CUSTOM5=1-4 (Psygore's speed modes) now
 select other options; use CUSTOM5=1 for the default or 8 for his normal mode.

 History:
 --------
 Version 2.0b (17.11.2026) by Vesuri:
 - CD32 edition: every change works in English and German. Disk edition: the
   changes apply to the English SPS #1657 version; other disk versions keep
   the 1.0 behaviour.
 - Optional enhanced framerate with smoother flight, cameras and ship effects.
 - World speed option: flight and the intro at 1.0x (10 updates per second),
   1.2x, 1.5x or 2.0x, or CPU-dependent as in the original.
 - Consistent game speed on faster processors and smoother cinematic movement.
 - Retimed intro, room, briefing, launch, landing, death and funeral animations.
 - Training Simulator: the Get Ready text zooms in smoothly with the enhanced
   framerate.
 - Improved projectile appearance and asteroid/mine hit detection; the
   player's cannon fire, asteroids and mines move evenly between updates.
 - Waypoint markers stay visible in front of nebulae in every mode.
 - More responsive room controls with limited cursor speed.
 - CD32 edition: a saved pilot appears in the bunk at once, not only after the
   next reload (thanks hexaae!).
 - Choosing a Training Simulator opponent with keys 1-4 shows the right ship
   (thanks hexaae!).
 - CD32 edition: one mouse click chooses a Training Simulator opponent; the
   game often lost the click before.
 - CD32 edition: Training Simulator high scores are kept with each saved pilot
   (thanks hexaae!).
 - Mouse control of the room and menu pointer, and on CD32 of the name entry
   box, where the right button is Return; the left mouse button works as fire. The first click after entering a
   screen is no longer ignored (thanks hexaae!).
 - Mouse flight: the mouse steers as in the PC version, and the left button
   fires while held (thanks hexaae!).
 - Mouse flight: the right button changes speed and rolls, a right
   double-click fires the afterburner, and both buttons or the middle button
   fire missiles, as on the PC (thanks hexaae!).
 - The right mouse button skips cutscenes, as Esc does.
 - Mouse speed option, 0.5x to 4.0x, or the mouse off (thanks hexaae!).
 - Enhanced framerate: rotating ships and the title logo keep their centre
   instead of shaking as they turn, and a ship at the edge of the view no
   longer shifts by a pixel.
 - Enhanced framerate: the ship on the TrainSim enemy details screen turns
   smoothly, and the screen no longer slows down while the details appear (on
   the disk edition, with a 68020 or better and Fast memory).
 - Enhanced framerate: cannon fire from ships wider than the Hornet shows from
   the guns, as the Hornet's does, instead of appearing well inside the screen.
 - Disk edition: faster arithmetic and collision checks in every mode, including
   the Amiga 500, without changing game behaviour.
 - Enhanced framerate at 1.0x world speed and caches enabled in newly
   installed icons; the original game remains available.

Version 1.0 (11.01.2014) by Psygore:
 - Nice color/newicon (created by Frank/Dr.Zarkov) and BoxIcon included
 - Some accesses fault patched
 - CD32 hardware chunky to planar (AKIKO chipset) replaced by software
 - Bugs in music replay routine fixed (thanks to Don Adan for the info)
 - Lowlevel and nonvolatile libraries functions patched
 - Manual and quick doc included
 - Quit with '*' (PrtSc)

